1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Here's a problem that bites builders. You write an instruction, it works in one place, and then it

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,000
behaves differently somewhere else.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Nothing errored. The instructions just landed in a different spot.

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:18,000
This lesson covers why that happens, starting from one key fact about clod.md, and how to keep your instructions portable.

5
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Let's line up the three places an instruction can live, because portability starts with knowing them apart.

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,000
First, the system prompt.

7
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,000
It's the top-level channel for standing behavior. On the raw API,

8
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:37,000
you set it directly, and things like output styles, the clod code feature that swaps the system

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:38,000
prompt's style section, and

10
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,000
the append option modify it directly, too.

11
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Second, clod.md.

12
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,000
These are developer-authored rules loaded into context, and here's the key detail.

13
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:54,000
They arrive as a user message alongside whichever system prompt you chose.

14
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Third, an in-session message. An instruction you type mid-conversation.

15
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
It steers the current session, but it persists nowhere once that session ends.

16
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Same words, three very different lifespans, and three different homes.

17
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,000
This is the key fact this whole lesson hangs on, and

18
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Anthropic documents it in both the clod code and agent SDK docs, so let's lock it in.

19
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Clod.md isn't part of the system prompt. When it loads, its content is injected into the

20
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:28,000
conversation as a user message,

21
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:35,000
delivered after the system prompt, rather than baked into it. That means it shapes behavior

22
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:42,000
alongside whichever system prompt you happen to choose, and notably, its content doesn't affect the system prompt cache.

23
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:49,000
Contrast that with output styles, and the append option, which do modify the system prompt directly.

24
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:55,000
So if you were picturing clod.md as system-level text, correct the mental model now.

25
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:57,000
It's a user message that rides along.

26
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:04,000
Let's put the two mechanisms side-by-side, because the difference decides where your instruction

27
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,000
actually takes effect. On

28
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:09,000
one side, things that write to the system prompt.

29
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,000
Output styles and the append option write directly into it.

30
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:20,000
They change the cached prefix and apply as top-level behavior for the whole run. On the other side,

31
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,000
clod.md, which rides as a user message.

32
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
It loads beside your chosen system prompt, not inside it, and it leaves the system prompt cache untouched.

33
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Why does this matter to you as a builder? Because if you need something to behave as a genuine system-level rule,

34
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:41,000
append it, or use an output style. If you want portable project rules,

35
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:46,000
clod.md is the right home, as long as you remember it's a user message.

36
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:52,000
So, where does drift actually come from? Not from the model quietly changing its mind,

37
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:57,000
it comes from the same words loading in different places on different surfaces.

38
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:05,000
Picture it. One surface reads a clod.md file off-disk that the next surface never even looks for.

39
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:11,000
A rule you set in a hosted chat app stays locked to that chat app and never travels to your code.

40
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:18,000
The agent SDK's minimal default prompt simply omits the standing guidance you assumed was always there.

41
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:22,000
In every case, the instruction text didn't change. Its placement did, and

42
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:28,000
placement is exactly what determines whether the model ever sees the instruction at all.

43
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,000
Same words, different home, different behavior.

44
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:37,000
Let's turn all of this into a short portability checklist you can actually apply.

45
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:43,000
First, state your rules explicitly and put them where the surface will really load them, instead of

46
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:45,000
assuming some autoload will handle it.

47
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:52,000
Second, commit your shared rules. Durable project rules belong in a committed clod.md,

48
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:57,000
so every teammate and every surface reads the exact same thing from version control.

49
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:05,000
Third, never trust defaults. If a rule genuinely matters, set it yourself rather than relying on a

50
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:07,000
surface to supply it for you.

51
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:12,000
Notice the theme running through all of these. You make the instructions home deliberate,

52
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:16,000
so the same behavior shows up no matter which surface loads it.

53
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:21,000
Let's make the surface scoping concrete because it's easy to forget in practice.

54
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Say you set a helpful rule in clod.ai.

55
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:31,000
That rule doesn't follow the model into your code. Your profile custom instructions and your project instructions

56
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:37,000
apply only inside that web app. Clod code and the raw API never see them full stop.

57
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:42,000
Each surface keeps its own separate configuration, and there's no shared pool

58
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:48,000
they all draw from. So the practical takeaway is this. If you want a rule to hold

59
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,000
everywhere, a chat profile is the wrong place to put it.

60
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:57,000
Portable rules belong in committed config like a project clod.md,

61
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:01,000
where every surface that reads config will pick them up the same way.

62
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:06,000
Let's close the loop with a quick guide to picking a surface for a given task.

63
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:13,000
When you're embedding clod inside an application and you want programmatic control over every request,

64
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:17,000
reach for the raw API or the SDK. When you're doing

65
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:23,000
repository-based development and you want your files, your tools, and your clod.md loaded for you,

66
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:27,000
automatically, clod code is the natural fit.

67
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:34,000
And when you just need quick interactive work, a bit of drafting, or a question, with no code, no files, and no

68
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:42,000
persistent integration, a hosted chat surface is the fastest path. The instruction you write should match the surface.

69
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:50,000
Programmatic rules in code, project rules in clod.md, and throwaway steering in the chat itself.

70
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:55,000
Let's end on a debugging move for when an instruction seems to get ignored.

71
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:02,000
Don't immediately assume the model is disobeying you. More often the rule simply never loaded on that surface.

72
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:06,000
So confirm first that the instruction actually reached the model at all.

73
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:12,000
Then check where it landed, whether it came in a system-level text, or as a user message,

74
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:15,000
because that changes how strongly it steers behavior.

75
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:22,000
Finally, retest the very same rule on a surface you fully control, like the raw API, where you can see

76
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:28,000
exactly what was sent. Almost always, an ignored rule turns out to be an unloaded rule.

77
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Fix the loading and the behavior you wanted comes right back.

78
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:37,000
Here's the bottom line. Portable instructions come down to one thing, placement.

79
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:42,000
It matters because the same words behave differently depending on where a surface loads them.

80
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:51,000
Just remember, clod.md rides as a user message, chat app rules stay put, and durable rules belong in committed

81
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:57,000
configuration files. That wraps up portable instructions across interfaces. When you're ready, I'll

82
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:58,000
see you in the next lesson.

